# Frontend Performance & Slowness

Use this when the site feels "extremely slow", when debugging SSR/API latency, or when adding new server-side data fetches.

## 1. Server-side API URL (critical)

**Problem:** If the frontend server (Next.js) calls the public API URL (e.g. `https://api.infinitycolor.co`) for every SSR request, each request pays for TLS handshake, DNS, and network round-trip. That can add hundreds of ms per request and make the site feel very slow.

**Solution:** Use the internal Strapi URL for all server-side fetches so requests stay on the same host or internal network.

- **Constant:** [apps/apps/frontend/src/constants/api.ts](apps/apps/frontend/src/constants/api.ts) – `STRAPI_INTERNAL_URL` is used only when `typeof window === "undefined"` (server). It falls back to `API_BASE_URL` if unset.
- **Env:** In production (e.g. Docker), set `STRAPI_INTERNAL_URL` to the backend reachable from the Next container (e.g. `http://backend:1337` or `http://host.docker.internal:1337`). See [backend-api-domain.mdc](backend-api-domain.mdc) and Docker networking docs.
- **Usage:** Homepage, PLP, and other server fetches already use `STRAPI_INTERNAL_URL` where appropriate. When adding new server-side `fetch()` to Strapi, use `STRAPI_INTERNAL_URL`, not `API_BASE_URL`.

## 2. Duplicate fetches

**Problem:** The same data (e.g. public settings) fetched twice in one page load doubles latency for that data.

**Solution:** Fetch once at the page level and pass data down to children that need it.

- **Example:** Home page fetches `getPublicSuperAdminSettings()` once and passes `homepageSettings` into `HomeProductSections`. `getHomepageSections(optionalSettings)` accepts optional settings and skips calling `getPublicSuperAdminSettings()` when provided. See [apps/apps/frontend/src/app/(product)/page.tsx](apps/apps/frontend/src/app/(product)/page.tsx) and [apps/apps/frontend/src/services/product/homepage.ts](apps/apps/frontend/src/services/product/homepage.ts).

## 3. Populate and payload size

**Problem:** Large `populate=*` or unnecessary relations (e.g. full Media gallery for list views) increase response size and Strapi/DB work.

**Solution:**

- **Homepage products:** Use `HOMEPAGE_PRODUCT_POPULATE` in [apps/apps/frontend/src/services/product/homepage.ts](apps/apps/frontend/src/services/product/homepage.ts) – CoverImage, category, variations, stock, discounts, colors only (no Media).
- **PLP server-side:** [apps/apps/frontend/src/app/(product)/plp/page.tsx](apps/apps/frontend/src/app/(product)/plp/page.tsx) – populate CoverImage, category, variations, stock, discounts, colors only (no Media). List view only needs one image per product.
- **PLP client-side:** [apps/apps/frontend/src/components/PLP/List.tsx](apps/apps/frontend/src/components/PLP/List.tsx) – Media is added only for desktop (`isDesktopForFetchRef.current`). Prefer not adding Media for list cards if CoverImage is enough.
- **Settings:** [apps/apps/frontend/src/services/super-admin/settings/public.ts](apps/apps/frontend/src/services/super-admin/settings/public.ts) uses `populate=*`. If the Strapi settings singleton has many relations, consider a dedicated public settings endpoint with limited populate on the backend.

## 4. Timeouts and revalidation

- **Timeouts:** [apps/apps/frontend/src/utils/fetchWithTimeout.ts](apps/apps/frontend/src/utils/fetchWithTimeout.ts) – PLP uses 15s by default. Long timeouts can make the site feel stuck when the API is slow.
- **Revalidation:** Homepage product sections use `revalidate: 90`, PLP uses `revalidate: 60`. Lower values mean more Strapi traffic; higher values mean slightly staler data but fewer requests.

## 5. Image optimization (FCP, LCP)

**Problem:** Oversized images (served larger than displayed) hurt FCP and LCP. Lighthouse flags "image larger than displayed dimensions" and "use responsive images".

**Solution:**

- **`sizes` attribute:** Always provide `sizes` on Next.js `<Image>` when using `fill` or responsive layouts. Without it, the browser assumes `100vw`, causing oversized downloads.
  - Desktop hero right banner (5/12 of ~1358px): `sizes="(max-width: 1280px) 50vw, 565px"`
  - Action banners (each ~half of 7/12): `sizes="(max-width: 1280px) 25vw, 400px"`
  - Mobile hero full width: `sizes="100vw"`
  - Product cards: match actual column width (e.g. `sizes="(max-width: 768px) 168px, (max-width: 1024px) 200px, 250px"` for ImageSlider).
- **Hero slide configs:** [apps/apps/frontend/src/components/Hero/config/slides/](apps/apps/frontend/src/components/Hero/config/slides/) – add `sizes` and set `width`/`height` to match rendered dimensions. Reduce oversized action banner dimensions (e.g. 800x900 → 400x500).
- **Static assets (CSS background):** Images loaded via `background-image` bypass Next.js Image optimization. Prefer WebP over PNG; convert with `sharp` and place in `public/images/`. Update slide configs to reference `.webp` path.
- **Next.js 16:** `images.qualities` defaults to `[75]`; add `qualities: [50, 70, 75, 100]` in `next.config` if using `quality={70}`. Use `preload={true}` for LCP images (replaces deprecated `priority`).
- **Lazy-load:** Use `loading="lazy"` and `priority={false}` for below-fold images. Hero first slide: `priority={true}` or `preload={true}`. Blog/category carousels: `priority={index === 0}`.

## 6. PLP-specific optimizations (avoid duplicate work)

- **Category validation**: PLP page and `generateMetadata` both need category validation. Use `getValidatedCategoryCached` from [apps/apps/frontend/src/utils/category-validation.ts](apps/apps/frontend/src/utils/category-validation.ts) (React `cache()`) so validation runs once per request.
- **Client products fetch**: On initial load the server already fetches products. [PLP/List.tsx](apps/apps/frontend/src/components/PLP/List.tsx) skips the first `fetchProducts()` run on mount (`didInitialMountRef`) so the same request is not sent twice. Client fetch runs when the user changes filters/page/sort.
- **Client categories fetch**: When the server passes `allCategories` (e.g. category PLP), the client uses them for the filter dropdown and does not call `getProductCategories` again. Only when visiting `/plp` with no category does the client fetch categories for the filter.

## 7. Load regression (e.g. scaling 6→12 instances, cache handler off)

If the frontend times out under load (e.g. ~1k VUs) and the **custom cache handler is already disabled**, the cause is elsewhere. See **[frontend-load-regression.mdc](frontend-load-regression.mdc)** for:

- Multiple instances with in-memory cache only → revalidation storm and Strapi overload
- Nginx `ip_hash` concentrating load on few instances during single-IP load tests
- Homepage fan-out (5+ Strapi calls per GET /) and how to verify Strapi capacity
- Checklist and fixes (shared cache, revalidate, Nginx balancing, keepAliveTimeout)

## 8. Checklist when the site is slow

- [ ] Is `STRAPI_INTERNAL_URL` set in the environment where Next.js runs (e.g. Docker/prod)?
- [ ] Are we fetching the same data twice in one request (e.g. settings)? Pass from parent when possible.
- [ ] Are product list requests asking for more than CoverImage + category + variations (e.g. full Media)? Trim populate for list views.
- [ ] Do hero and product images have appropriate `sizes` to avoid oversized downloads?
- [ ] PLP: Is category validation cached (getValidatedCategoryCached)? Is initial client products fetch skipped? Are server-passed categories used when available?
- [ ] Check apps/backend/Strapi and DB performance (indexes, Redis if used, response caching). See [backend-reports.mdc](backend-reports.mdc) for cache headers and [product-search-api.mdc](product-search-api.mdc) for search caching.

